--- §6  TWO UPPER BOUNDS (D)
    (i) PERFECT OVERLAP.  If every resource below could run
        concurrently with every other with no dependency and no
        arbitration, the token could not be shorter than the
        BUSIEST single resource.  'Perfect' therefore assumes:
        no data dependency between a weight stream and the layer
        command that consumes it, no shared bus, no re-ordering
        cost, and the same per-resource busy time as measured.
        It is an UPPER BOUND ON THE SAVING, not a design target.
        union                32,477,703 cyc/token =  129.911 ms
      (i-a) THE FORMULA LITERALLY, over the instrumented classes:
        busiest class        21,734,076 cyc/token =   86.936 ms   (mover engine)
        saving bound         10,743,627 cyc/token =   42.975 ms  = 32.77 % of the token
        (the sequencer-only residue 306,731 cyc = 1.227 ms
         is NOT in the union and no overlap removes it)

      (i-b) AND THE SAME FORMULA OVER RESOURCES, WHICH IS THE ONE
        TO READ.  (i-a) is bound by `mover engine`, and that class
        is `mv_busy`, which stands for the whole of a FENCE drain
        as well as for real mover work — a WAIT, not a resource
        doing anything.  Splitting it at the signature level into
        MOVER-and-nothing-streaming (real MOVX/MOVY/LDC traffic)
        and MOVER-while-streaming (the FENCE wait, whose resource
        is the weight path, already counted) gives the resources
        that actually occupy hardware:
          weight streaming (any chan)                 13,601,885 cyc =   54.408 ms  41.49 %
          layer compute                               10,402,465 cyc =   41.610 ms  31.73 %
          burst fabric                                 8,334,361 cyc =   33.337 ms  25.42 %
          mover work (MOVER, nothing streaming)        8,136,299 cyc =   32.545 ms  24.82 %
          layer state-DMA                              1,292,900 cyc =    5.172 ms   3.94 %
          state window                                   889,458 cyc =    3.558 ms   2.71 %
          record/LDC/EMB DDR                             361,840 cyc =    1.447 ms   1.10 %
          (for reference, MOVER while a channel streams — the
           FENCE wait — is 13,597,777 cyc = 54.391 ms;
           it is a wait on the weight path and is NOT a resource)
        BUSIEST RESOURCE     13,601,885 cyc/token =   54.408 ms   (weight streaming (any chan))
        SAVING BOUND         18,875,818 cyc/token =   75.503 ms  = 57.58 % of the token
        i.e. a token that is 131.138 ms could not, under
        perfect overlap, be shorter than 55.634 ms
        (busiest resource + the sequencer-only residue) — a
        speed-up of at most 2.36x.

    (ii) DDR-BOUND FLOOR.  The busiest channel's weight bytes at
         the beats/cycle THIS RUN measured while that channel was
         streaming — i.e. the floor this memory model imposes, not
         a floor the board imposes (WLAT 40, tb/tb_seq_chip.sv:69).
         chan 0: 16,030,080 beats at 1.1960 beats/cyc ->   13,402,775 cyc =   53.611 ms/token
         chan 1: 15,979,392 beats at 1.1960 beats/cyc ->   13,360,363 cyc =   53.441 ms/token
         chan 2: 15,962,496 beats at 1.1960 beats/cyc ->   13,346,295 cyc =   53.385 ms/token
         chan 3: 15,962,496 beats at 1.1960 beats/cyc ->   13,346,241 cyc =   53.385 ms/token
         BUSIEST is chan 0: floor 13,402,775 cyc = 53.611 ms/token = 40.88 % of the token

    (ii-b) AND WHAT THAT RATE IS, IN THE MODEL'S OWN UNITS.
         docs/QWEN35_NEXT_FEASIBILITY.md §4.2 (S) solves the 0.8B
         and 2B silicon steps for r = 1.1037 ui-cycles per 64 B
         beat = 17.40 GB/s/chan, with r = 1.000 called 'the
         engine's hard floor'.  This run measures its own r:
         chan 0: 1.1960 beats/aclk-cyc -> r = 1.0031 ui-cyc/beat = 19.14 GB/s
         chan 1: 1.1960 beats/aclk-cyc -> r = 1.0031 ui-cyc/beat = 19.14 GB/s
         chan 2: 1.1960 beats/aclk-cyc -> r = 1.0031 ui-cyc/beat = 19.14 GB/s
         chan 3: 1.1960 beats/aclk-cyc -> r = 1.0031 ui-cyc/beat = 19.14 GB/s
         So the TESTBENCH's weight path runs at r = 1.0031,
         essentially the engine's hard floor, against the r =
         1.1037 the model FITTED from two silicon steps — i.e.
         the TB's weight memory is 10.0 % faster than the rate
         the board's own measurements imply.  That is a REASON to
         expect this census's matvec term to be optimistic against
         the board, and it is stated as a reason, not a correction:
         `tb/seq_mem_file.sv` is a file-backed model with no
         refresh, no bank conflict and no read/write turnaround.

--- §7  RECONCILIATION
    (0) IS THE MODEL'S SERIAL STRUCTURE RIGHT?  The three terms
        the model sums, measured LIKE FOR LIKE here — weight
        streaming, mover WORK (not `mv_busy`), layer compute:
          weight streaming   54.408 + mover work   32.545 + layer compute   41.610
          =  128.563 ms against a measured token of  131.138 ms
          -> the three terms fill 98.04 % of the token, so they are
             very nearly DISJOINT: the model's SERIAL sum is the
             right shape for this design, and that is a MEASURED
             statement, not an assumption.

    (a) against the SERIAL model, docs/QWEN35_NEXT_FEASIBILITY.md
        §4.4's 9B W4 g128 row (S): matvec 58.95 + movers 32.66 +
        layer 47.00 = 138.61 ms/token.
        measured, this TB: matvec-engine-busy  54.460 ms (of which weight-streaming  54.408 ms),
                           mover-engine-busy  86.936 ms, layer-compute  41.610 ms
        their SUM 183.006 ms vs the measured token 131.138 ms -> overlap factor 1.3955
        AND THE MOVER TERM IS NOT MOVER WORK.  `mv_busy` is high
        for the whole of a FENCE too (mv_op 3, the drain wait),
        so the model's 'movers' term and this class are not the
        same quantity:
          mover WORK (MOVX+MVGO+MOVY)    8,137,008 cyc/token =  32.548 ms (24.82 %)
          FENCE drain WAIT              13,597,068 cyc/token =  54.388 ms (41.47 %)
    (b) against the BOARD's two lanes, evidence/qwen9b/g6/RD9_GATE.md §10.2 (S):
        L_LCYC 41.588 ms/token, L_SDMA_CYC 5.125 ms/token, the two
        together 34.1 % of the step and 65.9 % in NEITHER lane.
        measured here: layer compute  41.610 ms/token (31.73 %), state-DMA   5.172 ms/token ( 3.94 %)
        cycles in NEITHER lane: 21,918,200 = 87.673 ms/token = 66.86 % of the token
        (the board's 65.9 % is the same quantity measured on
         silicon; this is the TB's value for it, and the two are
         NOT the same instrument)

